# Copy to .env (NOT .env.local) and fill in once real values exist.
# The Prisma CLI reads .env only - a DATABASE_URL in .env.local produces
# "Environment variable not found" from a file that visibly contains it.
# Next.js reads .env too, so one file serves both. Both are gitignored.
#
# Every one of these
# is optional at build time - the site builds and runs correctly with none
# of them set (GA4/GSC wiring renders nothing; NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL falls
# back to a placeholder used in metadata/sitemap/robots).

# Canonical site URL, used in metadata, sitemap.xml, robots.txt, canonicals
# and every absolute URL in the structured data. No trailing slash.
# The domain is counterscent.com (renamed from parfumoza.com 2026-08-27 after
# a trademark-adjacent name collision; parfumoza was removed from Cloudflare
# 2026-09-10 and serves nothing). Set this the moment DNS points at the host -
# until then every absolute URL on the site emits an obvious placeholder,
# which is correct but must not reach production.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://counterscent.com

# GA4 measurement ID (e.g. G-XXXXXXXXXX). Leave unset and no GA script loads.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID=

# Google Search Console HTML meta-tag verification token. Leave unset and no
# verification meta tag renders.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GSC_VERIFICATION=

# NOTE: the public contact address (contact@counterscent.com, live since
# 2026-08-27) is NOT an env var. It is a constant in lib/site.ts, because a
# forgotten deployment setting would make /contact say "contact is not open
# yet" in production - worse than any alternative. Same reasoning for the
# site URL default above.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Marketplace / producer program
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE DATABASE IS NOW PROVISIONED (Neon, 2026-09-14); the payment rail is
# NOT, and does not need to be until a producer agrees to pay. This block
# once claimed more existed than did, so be precise about what is real:
#
#   REAL: a Neon Postgres project, 10 tables and 8 enums applied from
#         prisma/migrations/, confirmed by reading them back out of the
#         database rather than by trusting the migration's exit code.
#   NOT REAL: any code that connects to it at runtime. There is no producer
#         console yet (build steps 5-7), so nothing reads these strings
#         except the Prisma CLI on this machine.
#
# The public build still never reads DATABASE_URL and must not start: a
# paused or rotated database would fail the whole catalogue's deploy, and a
# credential would have to live in Cloudflare's build environment. See
# HANDOFF.md, "Run the export as a commit, not inside CI".
#
# The marketplace code is prisma/schema.prisma plus app/producers -
# there is NO app/api/subscribe and NO app/api/webhooks/stripe. Both were
# deleted 2026-08-27 with the Stripe integration and the static-export
# migration; `output: "export"` means this project has no API routes at all.
# What is here typechecks and is inert. See PRODUCER-PROGRAM.md §8 for the
# ordered list of what has to exist first. Creating these accounts is
# founder-side work - real business identity, bank and tax details that
# cannot be scripted.

# Postgres. NEON, decided 2026-09-14. See MARKETPLACE-PLAN.md §4.
#
# Why Neon and not Supabase, for THIS project specifically:
#   1. Supabase's free tier PAUSES a project after 7 days with no activity,
#      and a project left paused long enough is deleted. The producer console
#      will sit idle for weeks between sign-ups, so the normal state of this
#      database is 'nobody touched it recently' - it would spend most of its
#      life paused, and a producer trying to sign in would find it dead.
#      Neon suspends compute after 5 minutes and wakes on the next query.
#   2. The producer console is a CLOUDFLARE WORKER (HANDOFF.md, 'The
#      architecture answer'). A Worker cannot open an ordinary Postgres TCP
#      socket. Neon's driver speaks HTTP/WebSocket and has a first-party
#      Prisma adapter (@prisma/adapter-neon, supported since Prisma 5.4.2;
#      we are on 5.22). Supabase over the edge has no equivalent path.
#
# Neon hands you TWO strings for the same database. Both are needed and they
# are not interchangeable - see the datasource block in prisma/schema.prisma.
#
# DO NOT FILL THESE IN BY HAND. Run, from this directory:
#   neon link --project-id holy-sunset-91521586 --branch production -y
# It writes all three below into .env and refreshes them on later runs, so
# they cannot drift from the project. Requires `neon login` first.
#
# POOLED (hostname contains '-pooler'): what application code uses.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@ep-xxxx-pooler.REGION.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require
# DIRECT (same host WITHOUT '-pooler'): migrations only. Using the pooled URL
# here makes `prisma migrate` hang or fail without naming the real cause.
# prisma/schema.prisma reads this as directUrl. The name is Neon's rather than
# Prisma's usual DIRECT_URL because `neon link` maintains this one.
DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED=postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@ep-xxxx.REGION.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require
# Which Neon branch the two strings above point at. Written by `neon link`.
NEON_BRANCH=production
#
# NEVER COMMIT THE REAL VALUES. This repo is public. They belong in
# .env.local, which .gitignore covers via .env* (widened 2026-09-14 - the old
# .env*.local pattern left .env.production exposed). Anyone who reads a leaked
# string has full read/write on the producer database; rotate in the Neon
# dashboard if one ever escapes.
#
# `prisma validate` needs both set to *something* well-formed even offline,
# hence placeholders rather than empty values.

# Payment provider: PADDLE is the board's recommendation, and deliberately
# neither Stripe nor iyzico.
#   Stripe  - does not support Turkish accounts. The integration that once
#             lived here was deleted 2026-08-27.
#   iyzico  - capable of recurring billing, but the wrong shape for THIS
#             product: subscribers are US businesses, so a Turkish PSP means
#             FX friction on their side and leaves Sirketim as the seller of
#             record, owing US state sales tax, EU VAT and one e-fatura per
#             subscriber per period. It stays the right rail for Turkish
#             direct-invoice clients.
#   Paddle  - merchant of record, so it owes those taxes rather than us, and
#             our counterparty becomes one company with one document per
#             payout. Confirmed 2026-08-26 to onboard Turkish sellers.
# Open before any adapter is written: Paddle's marketplace acceptable-use
# policy, and whether it self-bills Turkish tax residents the way Awin
# explicitly does not.
# Nothing is needed here until a producer agrees to pay - the free tier
# requires no payment rail at all.
# See departments/accounting/reports/payment-rails-investigation.md.

# Auth.js. Generate a secret with: openssl rand -base64 32
AUTH_SECRET=
AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Local-only preview of the gated producer pages (/producers/submit), so the
# gated UI can be reviewed before authentication exists. Set to 1 and restart
# `npm run dev`.
#
# This CANNOT be enabled in a production build: lib/producer-session.ts also
# requires NODE_ENV === "development", which the bundler inlines at build
# time. An env-var-only switch here would be a genuine auth-bypass hole once
# this deploys. Pages using it render a visible preview banner.
PRODUCER_PREVIEW=
